@Tutorial(time: 10) {
    @Intro(title: "Match Arguments Precisely") {
        Stubbing `"apple"` and verifying `.any` is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: *any* amount, amounts *under* 100, codes *in* a range, collections *of a certain size* — or some predicate only you can express.

        In this tutorial you'll drive an `OrderService` through the full matcher vocabulary, then reach the arguments your code actually passed with `captured`.

        @Image(source: intro-matching, alt: "Three argument values flowing into a funnel, with only one passing through to the other side.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Match any — or exactly one") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Every argument position in a `when` or `verify` call takes an `ArgMatcher`. Two extremes cover most cases: `.any` matches everything, while `.equal` — or a plain literal, thanks to literal conformances — matches one exact value.

            @Image(source: chapter-matching, alt: "Two argument bubbles, one labeled any with many arrows passing through, one labeled equal with a single arrow.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create `OrderService.swift` with a protocol that prices orders in cents and validates item lists:

                @Code(name: "OrderService.swift", file: "02-1-1-order-service.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Start a test with just the mock in place, so you can watch the matchers accumulate one at a time.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-1-2-any-and-exact.swift", reset: true)
            }

            @Step {
                Register the broad stub first. `.any` matches every `Int`, so at this point every amount you ask about returns `100`.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-1-3-any-and-exact.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Now layer a specific stub on top. `999` narrows to `.equal(999)`, and because it's the more specific matcher it wins for that amount — while `199` still falls through to `.any`.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-1-4-any-and-exact.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The same literal sugar works in verifications: `verify(mock.discount(forAmountCents: 199))` counts only the calls that passed `199`.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-1-5-any-and-exact.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Optional arguments have their own matchers — `.notNil()` and `.nil()` — and they read naturally in both directions: `when(mock.validate(items: .any, coupon: .nil())).thenReturn(true)` stubs only coupon-less validations, while `verify(mock.validate(items: .any, coupon: .notNil())).called(1)` counts the rest.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Match with predicates and ranges") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            When the boundary matters more than the value, reach for comparison and range matchers. They mirror Swift's own vocabulary: `.lessThan`, `.greaterThan`, `.in(...)` with any range expression, and `.hasCount(in:)` for collections.

            @Image(source: chapter-matching, alt: "A number line with three shaded ranges labeled less than, in, and greater than.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a second test to the same class, starting from a fresh mock.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-2-1-predicates-and-ranges.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Build a discount ladder out of three non-overlapping bands. `.lessThan`, `.in(_:)` with a closed range, and `.greaterThan` between them cover every possible amount exactly once.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-2-2-predicates-and-ranges.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Probe one amount per band to prove each boundary routes where you expect.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-2-3-predicates-and-ranges.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                For everything else there's `.any(that:)`: your closure becomes the matcher, and it receives the real argument — an `Int`, not `Any` — so it type-checks like ordinary Swift. Here it counts round amounts: of the three calls above only `500` and `5000` are multiples of 100, so the assertion expects exactly two. `.hasCount(in:)` applies the same idea to a collection's length.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-2-4-predicates-and-ranges.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Capture and inspect arguments") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Matchers assert; sometimes you want to *examine*. `captured` replays each matching invocation through a closure, handing you the concrete arguments your production code passed — perfect for invariants that don't reduce to a single equality check.

            @Image(source: intro-matching, alt: "A magnifying glass hovering over two recorded invocations and their argument values.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Record two validations that differ in both arguments — one with a coupon, one without. A permissive `.any` stub lets both calls through so there's something to inspect.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-3-1-captured.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Narrow the capture to the coupon-bearing call, then assert against the concrete arguments. `items` arrives as a real `[String]` and `coupon` as a `String?` — no casting, and the compiler checks your assertions.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-3-2-captured.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Close the closure and the test. Filter and inspect compose freely: the matcher selects the invocations, `captured` shows you them. If no invocation matches, the verification reports a failed call count instead of silently skipping your assertions.

                @Code(name: "OrderServiceTests.swift", file: "02-3-3-captured.swift")
            }
        }
    }
}
